{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#148","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#148","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#148\",\"parent_revision\":\"16bbd7010720e947eeb54e6db383294a9c89838a47eca3177bccad0c97758c85\",\"root_revision\":\"16bbd7010720e947eeb54e6db383294a9c89838a47eca3177bccad0c97758c85\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Measured Baseline\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nFull suite: ~98s wall clock, 1278 passed / 10 skipped, on 4 physical cores (8 logical). Config is `addopts = [\\\"--cov-report=term-missing\\\", \\\"--cov=packages/skilllint\\\", \\\"-v\\\", \\\"-n\\\", \\\"auto\\\"]`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Three Findings Ranked By Cost\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### 1. Subprocess startup dominates \u2014 ~66s of ~98s\\n\\nOne `skilllint` CLI spawn costs **~1.05s**, measured three times (1.02 / 1.05 / 1.12). Bare Python plus `importlib.metadata` is **0.20s**, so **~0.85s is import**.\\n\\nThe suite spawns roughly **63 subprocesses**:\\n\\n```\\n$ grep -rc 'subprocess.run\\\\|subprocess.check\\\\|subprocess.Popen' packages/skilllint/tests/*.py tests/**/*.py\\npackages/skilllint/tests/test_external_tools.py:27\\npackages/skilllint/tests/test_plugin_structure_validator.py:17\\npackages/skilllint/tests/test_e2e_packaging.py:8\\npackages/skilllint/tests/test_provider_validation.py:6\\ntests/benchmarks/test_benchmark.py:3\\npackages/skilllint/tests/test_rule_fixtures.py:1\\npackages/skilllint/tests/test_external_scan_proof.py:1\\n```\\n\\n63 \u00d7 1.05s \u2248 **66s**, serialized within each worker.\\n\\n`packages/skilllint/version.py:43` runs `_get_hatch_version()` **at import time**, pulling in hatchling \u2192 `setuptools_scm` \u2192 `vcs_versioning` \u2192 a `git` subprocess. `python -X importtime` attributes **263ms** to `skilllint.version` alone, of an 851ms total for `skilllint.plugin_validator`:\\n\\n```\\nimport time:     24995 |     851384 | skilllint.plugin_validator\\nimport time:    101528 |     263134 |     skilllint.version\\nimport time:     31091 |      93622 |   git\\nimport time:       547 |      61063 |       httpx\\nimport time:      1824 |      63922 |   rich.markdown\\n```\\n\\nThis is the documented [hatch-vcs-footgun](https://github.com/maresb/hatch-vcs-footgun-example) pattern, deliberate so a dev checkout reports a live version. Nothing makes it lazy, so every import in every worker in every subprocess pays it.\\n\\n`httpx` (61ms, via `vendor_cache`) and `rich.markdown` (64ms) are also imported eagerly by `plugin_validator`.\\n\\n### 2. The `slow` marker never fires \u2014 23\u201345s\\n\\n`packages/skilllint/tests/test_e2e_packaging.py:7` states:\\n\\n> Tests are marked with @pytest.mark.slow to avoid running on every pytest invocation\\n\\n`addopts` never passes `-m \\\"not slow\\\"`, so all 10 wheel-build/venv-create tests run on every local invocation. Measured `-m \\\"not slow\\\"` saving: 23s and 45s across two reps.\\n\\nThe intent is documented; the configuration does not implement it.\\n\\n### 3. Coverage is not the bottleneck\\n\\n`--no-cov` measured 97.6s and 98.5s against a default of 95.3s and 114.9s. `nocov` was itself stable to \u00b10.9%, so coverage costs approximately nothing. Recorded because it is the usual first guess and it is wrong here.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurement Validity\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThat sweep ran while another agent was working on this machine; rep-to-rep spread reached **48%** on one config. Only conclusions robust to that noise are stated above \u2014 the per-invocation startup cost, the import breakdown, and the marker mismatch were each measured independently of the sweep. **Absolute suite wall-clock numbers in this issue are not yet trustworthy.**\\n\\nTwo sweep configs also failed outright (`rc=4`): `-p no:xdist` errors while `addopts` still passes `-n auto`. Serial baselines need `-n 0`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Remaining Measurement Program\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRun on a quiet machine, serialized \u2014 concurrent pytest invocations contend for cores and xdist workers and make every number meaningless.\\n\\n1. Serial baseline with the flag fixed, \u22653 reps.\\n2. Worker sweep `-n 0/2/4/6/8/auto`. `auto` picks **8 workers on 4 physical cores**; each worker pays the 0.85s import, so oversubscription is plausible.\\n3. `--durations=50` to rank hot tests.\\n4. A/B the import-time version detection.\\n5. A/B `_isolate_rule_registry` (`tests/conftest.py:24`) \u2014 `autouse=True` and function-scoped across all 1278 tests, and being autouse it also blocks anything above it from going session-scoped.\\n6. Subprocess-vs-in-process inventory. 98 `CliRunner` calls already exist in `test_cli.py`, so the in-process pattern is established; the open question is which of the 63 subprocess tests genuinely need a process boundary.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Rough Projection\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nFixing 1 and 2 alone: ~98s \u2192 ~35s, before touching parallelism.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- #128 \u2014 `RULE_REGISTRY.clear()` order-dependence, which constrains how far parallelism can be pushed\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"a43340d136d32d63974d9c4963a8888d4fc6d91a1d6ef2c594907bf7dc545a47\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDORXxKvc8AAAABXP-wDg\"}"}